1
00:00:00,967 --> 00:00:05,477
One extra node that makes linked
list code simpler: the dummy head.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,352
Merging two sorted linked lists.

3
00:00:08,402 --> 00:00:11,312
Before the loop can
start, you need a head.

4
00:00:11,362 --> 00:00:15,510
So you compare the two fronts
once, by hand, just to pick it.

5
00:00:15,833 --> 00:00:18,535
Then the loop makes the
same comparison again.

6
00:00:18,585 --> 00:00:22,183
And you still need an extra
check, in case one list is empty.

7
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:27,410
Now put one placeholder node in
front: a dummy. Tail starts on it.

8
00:00:27,733 --> 00:00:30,076
The first node is no longer special.

9
00:00:30,126 --> 00:00:32,734
The loop links it like every other node.

10
00:00:32,784 --> 00:00:36,323
The duplicate code is gone,
and empty lists just work.

11
00:00:36,667 --> 00:00:41,017
At the end, return dummy's
next. That is the real head.

12
00:00:41,367 --> 00:00:46,928
Rule of thumb: use a dummy whenever the
head of the result is not known up front,

13
00:00:46,978 --> 00:00:48,037
or might change.

14
00:00:48,367 --> 00:00:53,815
You will see it again in remove the n-th
node, partition list, and add two numbers.

15
00:00:53,865 --> 00:00:56,797
The full merge walkthrough
is on the channel.
